1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,680
Two B asks you to manage complex scenes and recognize abuse while
remaining a clinician. The patient may need urgent treatment, the
surroundings may contain evidence, and someone may remain at risk
after you leave.

2
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:26,808
These responsibilities are coordinated, not performed as three
disconnected checklists. Plan a safe route to care, avoid needless
disturbance and communicate concerns through the appropriate channel.

3
00:00:26,808 --> 00:00:37,656
The school module supplies the teaching framework. Current British
Columbia reporting law is identified separately where a broad
classroom statement needs a qualification.

4
00:00:37,656 --> 00:00:49,440
You are not asked to prove a crime or decide who is guilty. Your job
is appropriate care, safety, recognition, accurate records and the
required reporting or safeguarding action.

5
00:00:49,440 --> 00:01:01,848
A complex scene is one where clinical, environmental, interpersonal or
legal factors interact. A quiet home can be complex if an impaired
patient depends on a controlling caregiver.

6
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:12,744
Do not equate complexity only with a dramatic collision. Unclear
information, limited access and disagreement about who can speak for
the patient can materially affect care.

7
00:01:12,744 --> 00:01:24,336
Identify what is making this particular scene difficult and how it
changes the plan. A generic statement that the scene is safe does not
explain a specific unresolved hazard.

8
00:01:24,336 --> 00:01:37,224
Ask early for the resources that can address the actual problem.
Additional clinicians, fire, police or another service are useful when
their role is clear, not merely because more people feels reassuring.

9
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:50,952
Observe the approach and the surroundings before committing personnel
or equipment. Consider traffic, fire, unstable structures, hazardous
substances, weapons and escalating behavior as relevant.

10
00:01:50,952 --> 00:02:02,208
Estimate patient number and immediate clinical urgency without
confusing a distant impression with a completed assessment. Identify
obstacles to reaching and moving the patient.

11
00:02:02,208 --> 00:02:13,632
Identify who is already managing hazards and how the crew will
communicate with them. An agency being present does not automatically
mean every relevant risk has been controlled.

12
00:02:13,632 --> 00:02:24,648
State the first safe care plan and an exit or withdrawal option.
Reassess after entry because the important hazard may only become
visible when you reach the patient.

13
00:02:24,648 --> 00:02:35,880
In an original example, a kitchen initially appears calm. During
assessment a distressed relative stands across the only exit and
begins shouting at the patient.

14
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:46,608
The initial size-up is now out of date. Reassess access, behavior,
patient vulnerability and the ability to continue safely; tell the
partner what has changed.

15
00:02:46,608 --> 00:02:58,200
Use calm communication and appropriate support without confronting the
person about suspected guilt. If there is an immediate threat,
prioritize safety and request the appropriate assistance.

16
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:09,048
The revised plan must include the patient, not only the crew.
Communicate any interruption or movement and arrange the safest
available continuation of care.

17
00:03:09,048 --> 00:03:21,768
Clarify the shared objective and the distinct roles. Paramedics manage
clinical care, while other agencies contribute their own safety,
access or investigative expertise.

18
00:03:21,768 --> 00:03:33,432
Explain urgent patient needs clearly, including what movement or
access cannot wait. Listen to hazards identified by others and
negotiate the least disruptive safe route.

19
00:03:33,432 --> 00:03:45,456
Use a named contact and closed-loop requests where possible. If you
need police to manage a threat or fire to isolate machinery, confirm
the action rather than assuming your request was heard.

20
00:03:45,456 --> 00:03:58,176
Respectful collaboration does not require agreement with every
instruction. Identify an unsafe conflict directly and obtain
appropriate leadership support while maintaining the clinical
priority.

21
00:03:58,176 --> 00:04:11,976
Forensic awareness means recognizing that objects, clothing, body
fluids and original positions may later help explain an event. It does
not mean leaving a patient untreated to preserve a perfect scene.

22
00:04:11,976 --> 00:04:23,064
Choose a safe route that avoids unnecessary disturbance where
practical. Limit extra people and equipment in relevant areas without
obstructing required care or egress.

23
00:04:23,064 --> 00:04:33,480
When airway access, bleeding control or patient movement is necessary,
provide it. Note relevant original positions if feasible and
communicate what care changed.

24
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:46,824
Patient dignity and crew safety remain active considerations. Evidence
preservation is part of the plan, not a reason to expose the patient
unnecessarily or handle a dangerous object.

25
00:04:46,824 --> 00:05:00,360
Observe relevant objects and their position when it is safe and
clinically feasible. A medicine container, a damaged garment or a
displaced item may be important, but you are not conducting a scene
search.

26
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:12,576
Do not unnecessarily clean up, discard containers, rearrange objects
or handle a weapon. A dangerous object belongs in the appropriate
safety and law-enforcement process.

27
00:05:12,576 --> 00:05:24,504
If clothing must be cut, preserve relevant stains or existing defects
when this does not compromise care. Explain what you cut or moved and
follow the approved preservation procedure.

28
00:05:24,504 --> 00:05:37,272
The saved module mentions leaving clothing where it falls. Do not
universalize that phrase: safety, dignity and the actual evidence-
transfer process still determine how items are managed.

29
00:05:37,272 --> 00:05:47,160
A useful record distinguishes what you originally observed from the
state after treatment. Do not reconstruct an original position that no
crew member actually saw.

30
00:05:47,160 --> 00:06:00,288
Record the relevant alteration, the person making it when known and
the clinical or safety reason. For example, movement to obtain airway
access is a different fact from an unexplained moved object.

31
00:06:00,288 --> 00:06:11,376
Communicate significant changes to the appropriate investigator or
receiving clinician through the approved process. Documentation does
not replace time-sensitive verbal information.

32
00:06:11,376 --> 00:06:21,264
If you do not know who moved something, say so. Honest uncertainty is
more useful than a confident invented account that later conflicts
with the scene evidence.

33
00:06:21,264 --> 00:06:30,360
You may suspect assault while the possible assailant remains present.
Do not interrogate, accuse or attempt to settle the allegation at the
scene.

34
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:41,352
Maintain awareness of exits and changes in behavior. Use calm,
specific requests and avoid a contest over authority that makes the
situation more dangerous.

35
00:06:41,352 --> 00:06:54,480
Request the agency capable of addressing the actual threat. If care
cannot safely continue in the current position, use the appropriate
withdrawal or movement plan and communicate the clinical urgency.

36
00:06:54,480 --> 00:07:06,504
A dynamic risk assessment is more useful than the absolute statement
that every risk stops all care. Explain the hazard, the protective
action and how patient care will continue.

37
00:07:06,504 --> 00:07:19,704
Objective documentation does not mean deleting the patient's
experience. Record reported pain, fear and history with the source
clearly attributed, and distinguish them from your own observations.

38
00:07:19,704 --> 00:07:30,768
Use quotation marks only for words you can reproduce accurately.
Otherwise give an attributed paraphrase instead of making a polished
sentence look like a verbatim disclosure.

39
00:07:30,768 --> 00:07:44,184
A reasoned clinical impression or safeguarding concern belongs in the
record when identified as such and supported by facts. Do not turn
concern into a statement that a named person definitely committed
abuse.

40
00:07:44,184 --> 00:07:57,408
Describe an injury's location, appearance and relevant findings
without claiming its age from colour alone. An explanation that
differs between speakers is documented as differing accounts, not
proof of guilt.

41
00:07:57,408 --> 00:08:11,592
Abuse and neglect may be physical, sexual, emotional or financial, and
may involve unmet basic or health needs. The source emphasizes
patterns across history, findings and interaction.

42
00:08:11,592 --> 00:08:25,392
One sign is rarely specific. An injury, poor hygiene or a delayed call
can have several explanations, but a credible concern still requires
the appropriate assessment and reporting response.

43
00:08:25,392 --> 00:08:36,888
Keep the developmental and dependency context in view. A proposed
mechanism should be considered alongside the person's abilities, not
judged against an adult stereotype.

44
00:08:36,888 --> 00:08:48,576
Use nonjudgmental questions and consider access barriers. Ethnicity,
Indigenous identity, disability or poverty is not evidence that a
caregiver is abusive.

45
00:08:48,576 --> 00:09:01,752
The module identifies unexplained or concerning injury patterns, a
history that does not fit and delayed presentation as cues requiring
attention. None is a stand-alone test proving abuse.

46
00:09:01,752 --> 00:09:13,512
Ask whether the account fits the child's development and the observed
findings. Document the source of each explanation and relevant
differences without pressuring the child to resolve them.

47
00:09:13,512 --> 00:09:24,312
Notice fear, unusually restricted speech and whether necessary needs
are being met. Assess the child clinically and consider whether other
children may also be at risk.

48
00:09:24,312 --> 00:09:36,000
Do not use repeated leading questions or seek a confession. Provide
immediate care, record relevant spontaneous statements accurately and
make the appropriate protection report.

49
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:49,944
Older adults may present with injuries, pressure damage, poor
nutrition or medication mismanagement. These findings require
assessment and context, not an automatic attribution to intentional
neglect.

50
00:09:49,944 --> 00:10:02,064
A caregiver may answer every question, prevent private speech or
refuse necessary help on the person's behalf. Clarify who is making
the decision and whether they have actual authority.

51
00:10:02,064 --> 00:10:14,832
Fear, isolation and financial exploitation may be important even
without a dramatic injury. Use a safe opportunity to hear the patient
directly and identify what support they want.

52
00:10:14,832 --> 00:10:28,224
Age or a diagnosis alone does not establish incapacity. Assess the
relevant decision and communication needs while recognizing that an
adult may need safeguarding support without losing all autonomy.

53
00:10:28,224 --> 00:10:38,400
Intimate partner violence includes patterns of harm and coercive
control. An apparently attentive partner may still be preventing the
patient from speaking freely.

54
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:50,760
Changing explanations, repeated reported accidents, fear of leaving or
a controlling interaction can raise concern. They must be interpreted
with the person's account and clinical findings.

55
00:10:50,760 --> 00:11:02,232
Seek private conversation only when it can be done safely. A
confrontational demand that a partner leave may increase danger; use
the appropriate scene and support plan.

56
00:11:02,232 --> 00:11:14,952
Do not promise secrecy, force disclosure or make a blanket promise
that police will or will not be involved. Explain the relevant limits
and options without taking away a capable adult's voice.

57
00:11:14,952 --> 00:11:26,616
A person may depend on the same individual who controls access,
medication, money or communication. That dependency can explain why
help was not requested earlier.

58
00:11:26,616 --> 00:11:39,480
A disability or a different communication style is not itself proof of
incapacity. Provide appropriate communication support and assess the
actual decision rather than accepting a bystander's label.

59
00:11:39,480 --> 00:11:51,336
Ask what practical barrier prevents help: physical restraint,
inability to reach a phone, impaired decisions or fear of
consequences. The answer affects the safeguarding pathway.

60
00:11:51,336 --> 00:12:03,072
Keep the response proportionate and patient-centered. Protective
concern does not grant unlimited authority to detain, disclose
information or override a capable refusal.

61
00:12:03,072 --> 00:12:14,760
Introduce your role and ask permission for relevant questions. Use
ordinary language, a calm tone and an interpreter or communication aid
when needed and appropriate.

62
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:27,384
Begin with open, neutral questions rather than supplying a suspected
explanation. Listen to what the person actually says and avoid
repeated questioning that feels like an interrogation.

63
00:12:27,384 --> 00:12:37,128
Acknowledge distress without declaring that you know exactly what
happened. Explain what you can do now and the relevant limits on
confidentiality.

64
00:12:37,128 --> 00:12:48,144
Do not confront the suspected abuser or promise an outcome controlled
by another agency. Your credibility depends on honest scope, careful
listening and a clear next step.

65
00:12:48,144 --> 00:13:00,528
The reporting threshold is not proof beyond doubt. If there is reason
to believe that a child needs protection under the applicable
statutory circumstances, act promptly through the protection pathway.

66
00:13:00,528 --> 00:13:11,064
Separate the basis for concern from the conclusion. Describe the
findings and accounts that led you to report; the receiving protection
service assesses the information.

67
00:13:11,064 --> 00:13:23,904
A supervisor can provide useful support, but a message to a supervisor
is not by itself the statutory report to the appropriate protection
recipient. Do not let consultation create avoidable delay.

68
00:13:23,904 --> 00:13:34,128
Continue urgent care in parallel. Do not postpone lifesaving treatment
to obtain a detailed statement, image or a perfectly complete
reporting form.

69
00:13:34,128 --> 00:13:44,736
The provincial reporting page covers children and youth under
nineteen. It says you do not need certainty or every detail before
contacting Provincial Centralized Screening.

70
00:13:44,736 --> 00:13:59,664
The checked number is one, eight hundred, six six three, nine one two
two, available at all times. In immediate danger call nine one one or
local police first, then also make the protection report.

71
00:13:59,664 --> 00:14:11,688
Share the relevant information you actually know and identify
uncertainties. Do not delay solely to obtain missing demographic
details or to persuade a caregiver to agree.

72
00:14:11,688 --> 00:14:24,528
Record the reporting recipient and time, the concern conveyed and
relevant direction. A report is not automatically complete merely
because police attended or the patient reached an emergency
department.

73
00:14:24,528 --> 00:14:39,960
Current section fourteen includes a specific Indigenous-authority
provision. When its conditions are met, a report to that authority
with confirmation that it will assess the information removes the
duplicate provincial report requirement stated in subsection one.

74
00:14:39,960 --> 00:14:52,896
Do not assume that any informal community contact meets that
provision. Identify the applicable recipient and confirmation, or use
the appropriate current protection pathway without avoidable delay.

75
00:14:52,896 --> 00:15:06,096
Section thirteen also rejects protection conclusions based solely on
the listed socioeconomic conditions. Poverty, housing or parental
health alone does not establish the stated neglect grounds.

76
00:15:06,096 --> 00:15:18,192
These qualifications strengthen accurate reporting rather than
cancelling it. Document a real safety concern and its evidence without
turning identity or disadvantage into an allegation.

77
00:15:18,192 --> 00:15:33,024
Adult Guardianship Act Part Three addresses adults abused or neglected
who cannot seek assistance for its stated restraint, physical or
decision-affecting reasons. Section forty-six permits a report to a
designated agency.

78
00:15:33,024 --> 00:15:45,120
That is not the same statutory must as the child-protection rule.
Other professional, facility and organizational duties may still
require action and must be checked for the actual setting.

79
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:57,072
Use the relevant safeguarding and urgent-safety pathway, consult
appropriately and involve the adult in decisions. Do not turn a
possible report into assumed power to force care.

80
00:15:57,072 --> 00:16:11,952
When a capable adult declines support, clarify understanding, safety
and available options through the actual policy. When capacity or
immediate safety is uncertain, seek the required clinical and
safeguarding help.

81
00:16:11,952 --> 00:16:24,072
Reporting and confidentiality are not opposites. Use the legal and
organizational pathway that authorizes or requires the disclosure,
with information relevant to that purpose.

82
00:16:24,072 --> 00:16:35,880
The child-reporting law contains a confidentiality exception, subject
to its actual wording. This does not make every adult concern
automatically reportable to every person on scene.

83
00:16:35,880 --> 00:16:49,224
For BCEHS patient records, the official documentation standard
identifies FIPPA. The saved module's suggestion to review PIPA is not
a sufficient statement of the public-service privacy framework.

84
00:16:49,224 --> 00:17:04,176
Use secure approved channels and avoid informal photographs, social
posts or unnecessary bystander disclosure. A teaching discussion
should be de-identified and authorized, not a retelling of an
identifiable patient's story.

85
00:17:04,176 --> 00:17:15,288
Provide the identity and location you know, the immediate safety
concern and who is currently with the patient. Identify whether the
account is first-hand or came from another person.

86
00:17:15,288 --> 00:17:27,360
Describe relevant findings and statements without a theory presented
as fact. A concise account of why you are concerned is more useful
than a vague label such as bad family.

87
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:40,056
Include urgent care, the response and any planned destination or
immediate next step. Reporting must not leave the receiver assuming
that the person is safe when the crew is about to leave.

88
00:17:40,056 --> 00:17:52,392
Record the recipient, time and relevant advice, and clarify who owns
any follow-up. A request passed through several people is not
equivalent to confirmation that the correct service received it.

89
00:17:52,392 --> 00:18:03,744
The patient record explains the clinical encounter and relevant
protection actions. The actual protection report communicates the
concern to the legally or operationally appropriate recipient.

90
00:18:03,744 --> 00:18:14,760
PSLS is a separate learning and safety-reporting channel for the
events described in current A zero four. It is not the emergency
mechanism for a child in immediate danger.

91
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:27,312
A clinical error during the same call may require both patient
documentation and safety reporting, alongside a separate protection
report when that is indicated. Keep each record accurate for its
purpose.

92
00:18:27,312 --> 00:18:40,200
Do not omit an administered medicine or complication from the PCR
because it appears in an incident report. Equally, writing concern in
the PCR alone does not ensure the protection service was contacted.

93
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:51,912
In an original example, a nine-year-old has arm pain. The caregiver
describes a fall, while the child initially gives a different account
and then becomes silent when interrupted.

94
00:18:51,912 --> 00:19:04,800
Assess and treat the injury, maintain scene awareness and use a safe
opportunity for neutral questions. Do not accuse the caregiver, seek a
confession or repeatedly rehearse the child's account.

95
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:17,016
Document what each person said and what you observed, including
relevant interruption and findings. Identify the concern as a concern
and apply the prompt protection-report threshold.

96
00:19:17,016 --> 00:19:29,256
A plausible alternative explanation should be considered, but you do
not need to solve the allegation before reporting reasonable concern.
Continue clinical care and communicate remaining safety needs.

97
00:19:29,256 --> 00:19:41,304
An older adult is weak and the medication history is inconsistent. A
caregiver answers every question and says no hospital is needed, but
the patient has not been heard directly.

98
00:19:41,304 --> 00:19:52,272
Clarify the patient's wishes and decision-specific ability with
appropriate communication support. The caregiver's presence does not
prove authority to refuse on the person's behalf.

99
00:19:52,272 --> 00:20:05,016
Assess the clinical illness and the barriers to seeking help, and use
the relevant adult-safeguarding and consultation pathway. Do not force
the child-reporting algorithm onto every adult concern.

100
00:20:05,016 --> 00:20:18,072
Keep observation, reported history and impression separate in the
record. The aim is a defensible patient-centered plan, not a premature
label that the caregiver is guilty or the patient incapable.

101
00:20:18,072 --> 00:20:27,864
Lead the clinical handover with the active problem and current
condition. A safeguarding concern must not obscure an unstable
patient's immediate needs.

102
00:20:27,864 --> 00:20:39,144
Explain the relevant concern factually and identify its source. Avoid
repeating an allegation as an established diagnosis simply because it
has already been reported.

103
00:20:39,144 --> 00:20:50,544
Include treatment, response and any important changes to clothing,
position or objects needed for care. Tell the appropriate recipient
what may affect interpretation later.

104
00:20:50,544 --> 00:21:01,632
Confirm actual protection contacts and outstanding needs. A receiving
clinician hearing concern is not automatically a substitute for the
specific report you were required to make.

105
00:21:01,632 --> 00:21:14,112
A complex scene requires continuous assessment of clinical needs,
hazards, access and people. Complexity is a property of the
interacting problems, not the number of vehicles alone.

106
00:21:14,112 --> 00:21:24,888
Forensic awareness is compatible with urgent care. It means minimizing
avoidable disturbance and documenting necessary changes, not becoming
a police investigator.

107
00:21:24,888 --> 00:21:39,696
Mandatory reporting must identify the population, threshold, recipient
and timing. Adult safeguarding and child protection are related
responsibilities, but they do not share one identical statutory rule.

108
00:21:39,696 --> 00:21:53,544
An objective account remains clinically meaningful when it includes
attributed symptoms and reasoned impressions. It becomes unreliable
when interpretation, allegation and observation are silently blended.

109
00:21:53,544 --> 00:22:03,912
Be able to describe a complex scene and show how a new hazard changes
the plan. Name the resource you need and confirm the crew understands
the revised roles.

110
00:22:03,912 --> 00:22:13,416
Explain how you would provide necessary care while minimizing
evidentiary disturbance, and how you would document a clinically
necessary alteration.

111
00:22:13,416 --> 00:22:25,920
Recognize the module's child, elder, IPV and dependency cues without
turning one sign into a verdict. Use supportive communication and a
patient-centered clinical assessment.

112
00:22:25,920 --> 00:22:39,144
Finally, distinguish the actual child-protection duty from adult
safeguarding, preserve the privacy limits and document who received
the relevant information. The school's actual assessment remains
separate.
